#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\hspace{0.222em}-yz,\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1+x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048253 :topic/difficulty 0.6294524593639197 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429325 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 4143->1112. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429326 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 4143->315. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "8c8ae3a5-d4bd-50b8-9f66-a4a042bb870c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198392}] :topic/math-academy-id 4143 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049762}] :topic/title "Calculating Flux Through Cartesian Surfaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084 :topic/difficulty 0.7620038433200931 :topic/id #uuid "d952f888-5e27-5f27-a3ff-b83b56acd32a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275}] :topic/math-academy-id 2030 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016}] :topic/title "Double Integrals in Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548336 :question-generator/id #uuid "4b16d384-1b97-54b9-ace8-78618fbd0f73" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133927.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97d45ae-ae12-5c14-924c-d56fbb9046f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130939} {:answer/id #uuid "685918e6-ec50-5f06-acad-83ff00e3c712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521000} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfe55e2-32b3-566f-a4fb-ac15bcefa97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521012} {:answer/id #uuid "7903ed28-5d25-5da8-85e5-0a207c57c64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521035} {:answer/id #uuid "c351ba7c-4c6f-5399-9980-cd006f2d48fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c97d45ae-ae12-5c14-924c-d56fbb9046f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130939} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2becff-6366-549e-8db3-182572eab4ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130936}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee52e831-6d2f-5771-b93b-7f7fa07dc36b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161926" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2y⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96e8d31-77ad-5052-9b6a-9faf559523d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389231} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0e733a-0b54-50ae-a405-756c459c5edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389232} {:answer/id #uuid "615d7f91-8451-58fb-9b50-6912a6fa4329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389233} {:answer/id #uuid "2375212a-a45b-5bd5-94fc-2601931c58e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389234} {:answer/id #uuid "41c2840c-096c-53fe-8ab2-a18813e80463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb0e733a-0b54-50ae-a405-756c459c5edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389232} :answer-field/id #uuid "8986131b-decf-5b3d-8f27-aafe80a954f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389230}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d82e0bcb-c8b4-5aa2-9f3c-8a84246a499f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161927" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x+y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a292d0ec-0d72-578f-ac47-e9d6b19ac77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a292d0ec-0d72-578f-ac47-e9d6b19ac77c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234790} :answer-field/id #uuid "627ed6f9-5e55-5f96-a07b-04dde4b0b9fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b5155bc-b492-5617-9be0-0783e4c496d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-168625" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required circulation is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our top (tilted) surface ${S}_{T}$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2z-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2(4-x-y)-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2xy}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{8-2x-2y-{y}^{2}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{2y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{T}}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2xy)\\cdot (-1)-(8-2x-2y-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+2y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0f9cb64d0ae1c3615df5a05c9f580bb9c74f6529efd732cfd83e1f4328ff8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f463057-f805-5d6c-b391-8d33bfb0e1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278729} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f0c4c-50ad-5a82-9743-532f6b798a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,-2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279595} {:answer/id #uuid "77ebb165-94ff-572d-849d-60ea7ccc9364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279662} {:answer/id #uuid "ba62a950-4c28-54b0-850e-c0240391ff63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236} {:answer/id #uuid "dff98bc2-4344-5634-a1af-59494d77062c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,-2xy-1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba62a950-4c28-54b0-850e-c0240391ff63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280236} :answer-field/id #uuid "010aaa59-5dc6-5e8e-a83f-1b98c7c0cffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280837}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "240db9c9-76c2-5c97-8e69-9ca188d1d1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162361" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x-y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-x}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db0748b-118a-5639-b8f8-101df0972775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278719} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2fab1f-e285-51f4-a3b5-fff44aa9419e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279130} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0afc91-4186-596b-a560-07301b9b3b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279689} {:answer/id #uuid "820edc0c-36f3-5234-a0a6-bb7079f70fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279757} {:answer/id #uuid "8f71466e-d558-54ce-b28e-4bc0ccfab292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db0748b-118a-5639-b8f8-101df0972775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278719} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbd89c4-cb59-5b1c-a88e-c10bdb94a980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae18124e-1564-593a-8b3e-a861867113b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162366" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨-x{e}^{z},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x{e}^{4}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{4}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5032ce38-7144-56f3-8abf-062a36b12bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279325} {:answer/id #uuid "83b488ab-928c-5811-9c80-8d860a67a666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279771} {:answer/id #uuid "9b85bad0-7c0c-5cc8-b1a9-087dcba76ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279970} {:answer/id #uuid "e475d0cc-7991-51cb-aee6-efc92dbc2dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7ed414-d61d-5709-ad35-0b55e3209b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e475d0cc-7991-51cb-aee6-efc92dbc2dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e171a0c-c922-554a-8200-5911711795ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db5de703-3efe-5274-901b-09592c12a8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-162374" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩,$ and $C$  is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=x.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩{|}_{z=x} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 0+x))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3886a830-f807-532a-8dff-ac10e7ec4914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022} {:answer/id #uuid "7607b49a-d23b-55b8-8e3c-b7353e88eeeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372023} {:answer/id #uuid "c46e2782-3e03-5ada-93b7-ba596554275e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372024} {:answer/id #uuid "0f26e94b-0e7f-527e-a515-9f633c3e5b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372025} {:answer/id #uuid "5c50dc6b-1088-5cb6-baa6-a6a933d4f39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3886a830-f807-532a-8dff-ac10e7ec4914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022} :answer-field/id #uuid "10810889-3a2d-5477-b575-bc6677b334ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da6c973e-9e1d-506f-8988-7e41ce9a8511" :question/math-academy-id "q-162371" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}-{z}^{2}+1⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=-3.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}-{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=-3} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-6y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-8}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot 0-(-6y)\\cdot 0-8)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-8\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ with radius $3$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}-8\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=3} \\\\  & =-8(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (\\frac{9}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3a9c27-f34b-5594-94af-40a7c58297f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372029} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a89c10-18a7-59cb-8a1b-3a18b2a90938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372030} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ff01ff-40d6-5616-bd3a-9376f9415536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372031} {:answer/id #uuid "14cd568a-621b-5de0-baca-80df932e47d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} {:answer/id #uuid "daa9659f-982a-5882-a68f-e91cd62724ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14cd568a-621b-5de0-baca-80df932e47d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} :answer-field/id #uuid "5674f149-9ce3-5602-89ab-1339d40392d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372028}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00a16c1c-6465-5edc-becb-61f3932da901" :question/math-academy-id "q-162372" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨-y,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2x⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=2+2x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2x-y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-y,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2x⟩{|}_{z=2+2x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{2+2x-y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-2x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2)-(2+2x-y)\\cdot (-1)-2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2+2x-y-2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{2}+\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401337}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431147} {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} {:answer/id #uuid "54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431155} {:answer/id #uuid "c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431170} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} :answer-field/id #uuid "257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379" :question/math-academy-id "q-162373" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}-x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x+y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-x+y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} {:answer/id #uuid "3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431153} {:answer/id #uuid "83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431158} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431166} {:answer/id #uuid "da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162364" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $y=1+x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-{z}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4b66cf-ec3e-5130-bf78-dab2acbc861c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{x}\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520786} {:answer/id #uuid "3598a050-8338-5881-8c7e-00b829e53f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520878} {:answer/id #uuid "531bef77-5bda-58b0-b731-a0a09745931d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{x}\\,4+2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520953} {:answer/id #uuid "580a54b0-7193-5059-b069-221fd5506992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2+2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520963} {:answer/id #uuid "735bc584-a512-58b1-8842-88d84b62c851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735bc584-a512-58b1-8842-88d84b62c851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521022} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce8cadb-6f17-5458-a146-66e868571308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521421}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22c4166d-7812-5cc0-89d7-bc30157982f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162363" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ $y=\\pm 2.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{2}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab6fa70-a6cd-5cb7-9d73-3596ed01243a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548274} {:answer/id #uuid "19c9f542-d2e2-5968-bded-427e0d8db6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548281} {:answer/id #uuid "21f78820-0932-5d64-87e6-27386a744f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548285} {:answer/id #uuid "249d1b9e-afcc-5de7-be41-64c8e545e5c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548286} {:answer/id #uuid "728cf46f-850c-5bf2-8eee-8591a6dfdd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728cf46f-850c-5bf2-8eee-8591a6dfdd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548303} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe1fb6b-3741-520d-8e93-709e401053a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548332}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14caf42e-c7a2-5980-adda-a477fd9e1d63" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 9\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 9)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(81 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548275} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee86905-f590-503c-8720-85bf192e1221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(- 6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548277} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5c3ddf-a226-5fb1-8ac1-23c3bb6fc29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x - 2}\\left(- 6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548292} {:answer/id #uuid "53fef7c3-564b-508d-bbe5-96ce9d4af498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x - 2}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548297} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5aff36-c237-5c29-96f2-1540d685c947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(3 x^{2} + 6 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548275} :answer-field/id #uuid "279f0fe1-f8e4-5afa-b477-f2e27ef72c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548287}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $x - 2\\leq y\\leq 0$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=4 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle|_{z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(3 y\\right)\\left(2 x\\right)-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(4 y\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 6 x y)+(12 x y)+(3)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/667d3cc98a0951d2c6fa4be3684d0ac37c6c7307d2f38f56c9444593492e25f5.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0d836e-54a3-5340-9b13-dcc36232e947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548276} {:answer/id #uuid "2058e903-53c1-5c81-9185-9c31af4d09f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548284} {:answer/id #uuid "34c4690f-7fa6-5adf-99a3-e7e66e0f4675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548290} {:answer/id #uuid "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548311} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa3c0f4-aada-5874-bf25-4f4d4627107b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1111b70b-db9b-5b6e-800f-d01f0ede213c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2 - x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle|_{z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16\\right)\\left(- 2 x\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 2 x \\left(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} + 16\\right))+(0)+(0)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef88b67d11b3848190625d9329f220d0c213cb8252bd1d0de22ba8de035678b.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbae015-9c86-576e-9542-d658ec05d8b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548294} {:answer/id #uuid "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548313} {:answer/id #uuid "a5503768-564a-5162-9311-2202462dd105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548316} {:answer/id #uuid "b517f91d-49bb-51a2-bb5d-82c3c6c010ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548318} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e7d55b-c344-5ef3-a707-0a8e86cff164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548313} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19f56d4-a3af-5c69-a1b9-2d85f2264a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 6 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x + 18)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[18 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(18 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=18 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36fade4b-e6ad-561d-a3f5-aa58e1863d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 162 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548291} {:answer/id #uuid "5175042d-2d39-5599-87d4-e96b16f146f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 450 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548295} {:answer/id #uuid "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 612 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548308} {:answer/id #uuid "f64a0dba-2001-5ad1-8d0a-6f0446345425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 336 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548333} {:answer/id #uuid "fcabb8f4-cd17-54eb-81e2-ebbe693a7554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "612 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 612 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548308} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f987d97-8b0c-5075-a0a3-9441e76eccc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548293}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 x - 4\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle|_{z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} - 4 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 x - 4\\right)-\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 y + 4\\right)+\\left(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 x - 4\\right))+(\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 y + 4\\right))+(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[- 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) - 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) + 32 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(- 16 \\pi r^{3} + 16 r^{2} - 64 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(16 r^{2}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(16 \\pi r \\left(- r^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\pi r^{4} - 32 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(- 288 \\pi\\right)+\\left(- 324 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=- 612 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548327} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7db780-8908-5832-a157-32ca134dc1bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x - y + 3$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$ The circulation is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2}\\int_0^{2}\n$$ {{field-1}} $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x - y + 3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle|_{z=- x - y + 3}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x y\\right)\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(4 y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((4 x y)+(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)+(4 y)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1f/1f3f8869870631489863fa51c3b3297626962e265c43938fb1fa10273ec8e677.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064c81e9-cac4-5b54-aab3-071b92c05f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548271} {:answer/id #uuid "52609f73-07a8-520a-a473-dcfa11de11b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548296} {:answer/id #uuid "776a83db-0de1-5556-a705-63fc901967dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548304} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548314} {:answer/id #uuid "a13070d2-5fd5-5d85-a19f-8d85e967eafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548314} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb448220-651b-59de-aca5-26472ab8b179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548331}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=2}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 4)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(36 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=36 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006d6dfe-0844-5971-9c61-382cbb28d0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548270} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548273} {:answer/id #uuid "3028c6da-4535-5dec-ad67-803e3189ac38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548289} {:answer/id #uuid "5afa31f9-d938-51cb-b486-71ac3e40af68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548298} {:answer/id #uuid "90fcb849-8f04-5003-8a20-d247a6ce6d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548273} :answer-field/id #uuid "1194953f-36a6-55fd-86be-9f2bd4fd97cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548279}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6222ad0-cdab-5e3b-b25d-c7228bf6bc82" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=4$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(4\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(4\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=4}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 16\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 16)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[16 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(144 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=144 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b21800-0e88-5dbb-a9a1-66f5cccf128e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548272} {:answer/id #uuid "6b94038e-032c-5029-a77e-c204b7c5d549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548300} {:answer/id #uuid "804436a9-64c7-584d-bd22-73da3b39a9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548307} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f9c2ea-45a4-530b-a88e-c343c3142e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548322} {:answer/id #uuid "def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548328} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce7b7e5-6f3c-584c-bca6-6514b9a1b744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548335}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc0d6a0e-f5b6-5657-a4c8-70261c68ec76" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2 x.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x\\right)=2\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle|_{z=2 x}\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(0\\right)\\left(2\\right)-\\left(4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e90636-c836-54ff-81ae-058a934ee6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548288} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6cb252-46c3-558d-bcc6-d52eee217622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548309} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fe49c8-710f-5b92-9a08-bb94ad638ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548323} {:answer/id #uuid "d0eaa6cc-4368-55af-b243-68c7b79a2981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548325} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548330} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2f5e54-e210-5fdc-8326-8e2ab4e24cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e276ccb8-03fe-518f-a12f-012c8be3b237" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=1.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=1}\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 1)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[\\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(9 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=9 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067db027-125f-563d-954d-9e67b4fde190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617255} {:answer/id #uuid "34ca402b-b080-5fdc-883f-f6ad834bfbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617347} {:answer/id #uuid "896ef29a-68f5-587c-8d99-31ed6c6870ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617474} {:answer/id #uuid "c0646f5d-df70-507c-bc50-a604aa30aa73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,-4x-2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617568} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c6f33c-e850-5204-a0a0-b42ca512f797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,8x+2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896ef29a-68f5-587c-8d99-31ed6c6870ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617474} :answer-field/id #uuid "84a6c8a4-4db1-5fa0-a4bf-6c47d6502129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617714}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "852b8c26-61ec-5ce4-909a-b99005179ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162362" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder. One such surface is our half-ellipsoid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{-4x}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)-0\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eae72066363dd2c749f85fcc1568c15d65d193af83b9185ab084aef9a38db045.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}]}
